      "math_academy_id": "e-7703",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12.$",
      "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
